      "title": "Full 5-torsion of the regular-pentagon quintic pencil",
      "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23137834",
      "publication_date": "2026-10-04",
      "description": "<p>This research note computes all twenty-five geometric fifth-torsion points of the genus-one normalization of the regular-pentagon quintic pencil in McCallum's Question 17 in the AIM workshop problem list on rational and integral points. For an explicit equation normalization over K=Q(sqrt(5)), it gives a cubic model, both birational maps, a degree-ten residual polynomial and both ordinate branches. The complete division field is K(sqrt(5+2sqrt(5)),zeta_5,theta), where theta^5=lambda+phi^5 and phi=(1+sqrt(5))/2. Its degree is four or twenty, with the exact splitting criterion and Galois action specified. The computation covers every elliptic normalization, including the allowed cuspidal plane member.</p><p>The proof applies classical Tate normal form, Fisher's full-level modular cover, Verdure's full-torsion specialization criterion, Morton's prior explicit Tate torsion formulas and residual polynomial, division-polynomial theory and the Weil pairing. It credits the source's known infinity subgroup and the earlier partial computation. No first-discovery or first-priority claim is made. The verification supplement supplies portable exact symbolic and standard-library arithmetic controls, independent finite group-law checks, deliberate mutants, primary references and a bounded priority audit. Nonregular or star-polygon variants and Tate-Shafarevich or local-solubility constructions are outside the stated theorem.</p><p>This is an unrefereed preprint. AI tools were used extensively in derivation, computation, literature research, writing, reproduction and adversarial review. Automated reviews are not independent external human peer review; no such human review or formal proof-assistant certification is claimed.</p>",
